/* Function inversion by driving (E-15).

   Gluck and Turchin, *Application of Metasystem Transition to Function
   Inversion and Transformation* (ISSAC '90, pp. 153-158): given a program
   computing `y = <F x>`, synthesise `x = <F-Inverse y>` by driving the *forward*
   definition under an inverse configuration -- the input free, the output known.
   Object-level computation is unidirectional; the metasystem, which takes the
   process as a whole and builds its configuration graph, is bilateral, so
   "information flows backward from outputs to inputs just as easily as forward
   from inputs to outputs". As driving proceeds the output is disassembled
   pattern-by-pattern and the corresponding input is synthesised constructively.

   `Wrap` is the forward program: it encodes a text as a `Cons` spine closed by
   `Nil`, preserving every symbol. The inverse is *not written here* -- it is the
   artifact `refal invert examples/invert-list-encoder.ref Wrap` produces, and it
   is emitted, checked, and round-tripped by the gate in `check_examples.rs`.
   Writing the decoder by hand would defeat the demonstration.

   The forward program must be lossless for an inverse to exist at all: a step
   whose output drops a symbol destroys the information the inverse would need.
   That is a property of the program, not of the synthesizer, and it is why a
   run-length encoder is *not* the fixture this row uses. */

$EXTERN Prout;

/* Each command-line argument arrives as a bracket of its characters. */
$ENTRY Go {
  (e.Input) = <Prout <Wrap e.Input>>;
}

/* The forward program under inversion: encode a text as a Cons spine. */
Wrap {
  = 'Nil';
  s.Symbol e.Rest = 'Cons' s.Symbol <Wrap e.Rest>;
}
